{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788364088,"nonce":"hCsAsBDvRNkgchpnas1jiB52yOc=","title":"`designate`'s reach report is ALLOWED AREA only — a target inside the area can still be unreachable, and that is the same silence","message":"`b7359fa` shipped `designate`'s `reach` block: per target, over the roster of\ncolonists capable of the work, `RimWorld/ForbidUtility.InAllowedArea`. It closes\nthe case that killed `Marco`. It does **not** close the neighbouring one, and\nthe envelope says so in its own `test` field rather than letting the word\n\"reach\" be read as more than it is:\n\n    \"test\": \"ALLOWED AREA ONLY. This is not a pathing test: a target inside a\n             pawn's area may still be unreachable (no route, a closed region, a\n             locked door). Use `reachable {from, to, pawn}` for that question.\"\n\n**The failure mode is identical.** A `mine` designation behind an unbuilt wall,\na `hunt` on the far side of a river, a `haul` in a room whose only door is\nforbidden: the designation is valid and permanent, no pawn generates a job, no\nalert fires, and `accepted: N` is truthful and useless. It is the same sentence\nas the playbook lesson `a-designation-outside-the-allowed-area-does-nothing`,\none gate along.\n\n\nCost, and the number is the argument. The area test is one bool-array index per\n(target, pawn) — `Area.this[IntVec3]` is `innerGrid[cellIndices.CellToIndex(c)]`\n— so 20,000 cells x a ten-colonist roster is 200,000 array reads, cheaper than\nthe designator pass that produced the targets. `Pawn.CanReach` is a region\ntraversal through `Map.reachability`, and at the same ceiling that is a\ndifferent order of magnitude.\n\nTwo further things a worker must settle rather than assume:\n\n1. **`Reachability.CanReach` WRITES.** It populates a `ReachabilityCache`\n   keyed on (start region, dest, traverse parms). It is a memo, not scribed\n   state, and `PawnOrderVerbs.PrioritizeRejection` already calls `CanReach` on\n   the player-verb path — but the observers-never-mutate rule means the class\n   has to be named and placed in `PawnSafe`/`WorldSafe`'s taxonomy before it is\n   run 200,000 times, not after.\n2. **Per-pawn or per-region?** Reachability is a property of the region graph\n   plus the pawn's `TraverseParms`, so N pawns with the same parms share an\n   answer. Collapsing the roster to distinct `TraverseParms` before the loop is\n   probably what makes this affordable at all, and `SpatialVerbs.Reachable`\n   already builds them (`TraverseParms.For(pawn)`).\n\n\n1. `designate` reports, per target, whether any capable colonist can PATH to it,\n   distinctly from whether the area allows it — two different corrections\n   (build a door / extend the area) that must not read alike. The existing\n   `reach.test` field is rewritten to say what is now covered.\n2. It is bounded. A budget argument or a cell ceiling above which the pathing\n   half is skipped, with the envelope SAYING it was skipped rather than\n   reporting a clean pass it did not compute. (The silent-false-clean direction\n   is the one that cost a colonist; a named \"not checked\" is fine.)\n3. The `CanReach` cache write is classified in `PawnSafe.cs` or `WorldSafe.cs`\n   with the same rigour as the existing entries, and the class is named in the\n   verb's header.\n4. The refusal condition is re-derived: today a batch is refused when no capable\n   pawn's AREA allows any target. Whether \"no capable pawn can path to any\n   target\" joins it, or stays a report, is the worker's call to make and record\n   — the same call `b7359fa` made for the area half.\n5. `accept/\u003cid\u003e-designate-path.py` on a bench: designate inside a sealed room\n   with no door, assert the report distinguishes it from an area exclusion;\n   build the door, assert it clears.\n\nFound while implementing `b7359fa` (repair round, session 22). Not a defect in\nwhat shipped — the envelope names the limit — but the limit is a real hole an\nunattended run can fall into, and it is invisible from the outside.","files":null}]}